 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Replacement Policy
+
+Eviction uses 2Q. The cache holds a cold queue of sheets seen once and a hot queue of sheets that have been reused. Both queues are ordered by the time an entry entered the queue, and each queue is bounded by its byte share of the 12 GiB cache.
+
+- A completed sheet is admitted to the tail of the cold queue. If the cold queue exceeds its share, its head entries are evicted until it is within the share.
+- A request that hits a cold sheet promotes it to the tail of the hot queue. If the hot queue exceeds its share, its head entries are evicted until it is within the share.
+- A request that hits a hot sheet leaves its queue position unchanged.
+
+This keeps a one-pass screening from displacing the smaller set of sheets that editors compare repeatedly: screening sheets arrive in the cold queue and leave from its head, while reused sheets move to the hot queue and are protected by the hot share.
+
+The hot queue's share is a tuning parameter in worker configuration. It is not part of the HTTP contract, the render queue, or the disk budget.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy for the private preview cache | 2Q with a cold queue and a hot queue | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q retains reused comparison sheets while a sequential screening cycles through the cold queue, which is the dominant risk for this workload. LRU has less bookkeeping but a screening can evict the comparison set; aged frequency counts retain popular clips but require counter maintenance and aging and keep stale favorites after a project switch. All three policies are private to the worker and leave the HTTP contract, render queue, and disk budget unchanged, so 2Q's added bookkeeping stays inside the cache. |
+
